### Tested versions

Release: Godot Engine v4.3.stable.official.77dcf97d8 - https://godotengine.org
OpenGL API 3.3.0 NVIDIA 556.35 - Compatibility - Using Device: NVIDIA - NVIDIA GeForce RTX 3070 Laptop GPU


### System information

Godot v4.3.stable - Windows 10.0.19045 - GLES3 (Compatibility) - NVIDIA GeForce RTX 3070 Laptop GPU (NVIDIA; 32.0.15.5635) - 11th Gen Intel(R) Core(TM) i7-11850H @ 2.50GHz (16 Threads)

### Issue description


Given the following C++ code:

```c++
TypedArray<Dictionary> sigs = get_signal_list();
for(int i = 0; i < sigs.size(); ++i) {
	Dictionary signal_info = sigs[i];
	String signal_name = signal_info["name"];
	UtilityFunctions::print("Signal: ", signal_name);

	// Optionally, print additional signal details
	if(signal_info.has("args")) {
		Array args = signal_info["args"];
		UtilityFunctions::print("  Args: ", String::num(args.size()));
	}
}

auto child_order_changed = callable_mp(this, &GodotBinderHost::childOrderChanged);
r = worldNode->connect("child_order_changed ", child_order_changed);
if(r) {
    UtilityFunctions::print("Error connecting child_order_changed signal: %i", r);
}

auto child_exited_tree = callable_mp(this, &GodotBinderHost::childExitingTree);
r = worldNode->connect("child_exiting_tree", child_exited_tree);
if(r) {
	UtilityFunctions::print("Error connecting child_exiting_tree signal: %i", r);
}

```
I get the following output:
Signal: ready
  Args: 0
Signal: renamed
  Args: 0
Signal: tree_entered
  Args: 0
Signal: tree_exiting
  Args: 0
Signal: tree_exited
  Args: 0
Signal: child_entered_tree
  Args: 1
Signal: child_exiting_tree
  Args: 1
Signal: child_order_changed
  Args: 0
Signal: replacing_by
  Args: 1
Signal: editor_description_changed
  Args: 1
Signal: script_changed
  Args: 0
Signal: property_list_changed
  Args: 0
ERROR: In Object of type 'Node3D': Attempt to connect nonexistent signal 'child_order_changed ' to callable '<CallableCustom>'.
   at: (core/object/object.cpp:1390)

However, the signal does work if I connect to "child_order_changed" in gdscript. Additionally, other signals like: "child_entered_tree" and "tree_exiting" will connect and fire when I connect to the same Node3D in same method via C++.


### Steps to reproduce

Make a gdextension with a Node3D in the scene. Attempt to connect the "child_order_changed" signal to it as is shown above that is bound to a member function.

### Minimal reproduction project (MRP)

N/A
